arguments:
{"summary": "User James Lee (user ID: james_lee_6136) has reservation XEWRD9, a round-trip basic economy booking from LAX to ATL and back. The outbound flights (LAX→ORD on May 13, ORD→ATL on May 14) have already been flown. The return flights are scheduled for May 21 (ATL→DFW→LAX). 

The user wants to change the return destination from LAX to LAS (Las Vegas) and switch to a nonstop flight from ATL to LAS on May 21. This change is not permitted under standard modification rules because: (1) basic economy cannot be modified, (2) destination cannot be changed, and (3) part of the trip has already been flown.

The user is willing to upgrade to regular economy if it helps and is requesting to speak with a supervisor or rebooking desk to explore any possible exceptions or alternative options. The reservation has travel insurance and 2 passengers (James Lee and Mason Lopez)."}

{"summary": "Customer Noah Muller (user ID: noah_muller_9847, gold member) is requesting compensation for a delayed flight. Reservation 4OG6T3 is a round-trip basic economy booking from BOS to LAS with travel insurance. The outbound connecting flight HAT018 (SEA → LAS) on May 11, 2024 was delayed, which the customer says disrupted their arrival plans. 

The customer is requesting compensation for the delay but does not want to change or cancel the reservation. Per policy, delay compensation ($50/passenger) is only available when the customer changes or cancels the reservation. However, the reservation is basic economy (cannot be modified) and part of the trip has already been flown (cannot be cancelled by the agent). 

The customer feels this is unfair since the delay already impacted them and the booking restrictions prevent them from taking the actions that would qualify them for compensation. The customer initially claimed 3 passengers, but the reservation shows only 1 passenger (Noah Muller). Return flights (LAS → SEA → BOS) are scheduled for May 17, 2024."}
